WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.284
Have you ever wondered what made the&nbsp;
loudest sound on Earth?

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.620
To put things in perspective, let's start&nbsp;
small...

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.640
Breathing is around 10 decibels.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.046
Light rain comes in at 40 decibels.

00:00:16.046 --> 00:00:18.765
You get 70&nbsp;from a vacuum cleaner.

00:00:19.045 --> 00:00:21.433
90 from a blender.

00:00:21.591 --> 00:00:24.653
And a live rock band will produce around 120.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:29.839
The&nbsp;decibel scale measures noise logarithmically.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.805
For our non-mathematicians...

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:37.249
When a sound increases&nbsp;from 10 decibels to 20 decibels, it becomes 10 times more powerful.

00:00:37.249 --> 00:00:42.714
Going from 20 decibels to&nbsp;30 increases the power by 10 times again.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.140
Once you get into the 150 decibel range, the sound&nbsp;becomes powerful enough to rupture eardrums.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.783
So you should stand well clear of a starting&nbsp;pistol at 140 decibels

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.600
or a rocket launch of 180.

00:00:55.442 --> 00:01:01.500
And at 194 decibels, sound waves become shock&nbsp;waves which can destroy nearby buildings.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.983
But that's nothing compared to what happened&nbsp;on the 27th of August 1883.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:10.187
On the Indonesian island of Krakatoa, a volcanic eruption produced

00:01:10.187 --> 00:01:14.730
what scientists believe to be the loudest sound&nbsp;recorded in human history 

00:01:14.935 --> 00:01:16.962
at an estimated&nbsp;310 decibels.

00:01:17.492 --> 00:01:20.811
It was reportedly heard in more&nbsp;than 50 different locations 

00:01:20.876 --> 00:01:23.760
and the sound wave&nbsp;travelled three and a half times around the planet.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.820
For as many as five days after the explosion, weather stations observed an unprecedented&nbsp;spike in pressure

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.290
recurring roughly every 34 hours&nbsp;
as it circled the globe.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.880
It caused deadly tsunamis as far as Hawaii and South America;

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.503
major drops in&nbsp;temperature in the Northern Hemisphere,

00:01:40.503 --> 00:01:43.091
and deeply tinted sunsets in the West.

00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:49.253
Some scientists&nbsp;have theorized that Edvard Munch's 'The Scream' depicts those vibrant orange skies.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.435
Nothing in&nbsp;recorded history has ever come close.

